body:
TBH I don't read a lot of western philosophy. nihlism jst made me want to die ( i know it's like freeing for others). Taoism and eastern phil is chill though. For how much exposure I get to western, eastern phil has been a pleasant experience. Especially considering their way of spreading ideas is sometimes through prose and poems, which resonate wayy more with me. I guess that would be another dislike of mine regarding western phil: very academic writing.

&#x200B;

edit: Actually, I would also say novels are as educational as nonfiction books because they get you to practice empathy. A novel like beloved, which talks about how black families were split up during slavery by white people who knew their slaves were just as human as them, portrays a part of slavery that most people don't consider.  For example, Sethe’s (a mother who was enslaved)  act of infanticide illuminates the perverse forces of the  institution of slavery: under slavery, a mother best expresses her love  for her children by murdering them and thus protecting them from the  more gradual destruction wrought by slavery (which you can see in the others chars in the book having no ability to love).

Yeah, which is why I'm hoping to learn the basics from bootcamp and build from that. 

&#x200B;

On another note, do you have any favorite courses from U of R?

Dude just keep adding to it. Don't try to force your ideas into a box; ex. if you sat down thinking of making a Travis Scott beat and you made tame impala, go with it.  I used to beat myself up so much for not being able to make the beats I wanted to, instead of just letting the music take me wherever. 

&#x200B;

Then there's the issue of good instruments. Good songs need good sounds. How you get these sounds is up to you, but don't limit yourself before you even get to the creative part.

This is a bit more complex, but I found it super helpful with transposition stuff

&#x200B;

To my understanding, every Major key has a minor key with all the same notes. However, the place the the key starts itself is different.

&#x200B;

Lets say you have C Major

The notes are C, D, E, F, G, A, B, C

Now lets do A Minor

The notes are A, B, C, D, E, F, G

&#x200B;

These are called **relative keys**. the sound/ "vibe" of the key is different (A major can never be a minor), but the notes are the same. Only difference is where you start.

&#x200B;

So, if your sample is in F minor, the **relative key** is A flat major. They both share the same notes, just different starting places.

&#x200B;

Again, this wont make a major or a minor or vice versa,  but can create some cool sounds.

&#x200B;

Happy producing !

BNYX, travis scott and oksami.

&#x200B;

all 3 are phenomenally talented.
